From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rui Figueira <rfigueira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 11:43:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Share one gradient LUT across consecutive equal gradients
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every gradient fill pushes its own EncodedGradient, and each one
lazily builds its own colour lookup table. blitz-paint draws a
repeating gradient layer as one fill per tile, so a dot pattern
(`radial-gradient(#000 2px, transparent 2px) 0 0 / 8px 8px`) builds
the same LUT for every tile. A hard stop has a zero-width range,
so the LUT has the maximum 4096 entries: 16 KB as u8 and 64 KB as
f32, per tile. About 7,500 such tiles exhaust the renderer
isolate's 128 MB, and blog.cloudflare.com's 10px dot grid reaches
that once the viewport is 1536px or wider.

The LUT depends only on the stops and the interpolation settings,
which the gradient's cache key already holds. encode_into now
reuses the LUT cells of the most recent gradient when the keys
match. It compares only that gradient: the tiles of one layer are
consecutive, and a scan over every earlier paint would be O(n^2).

vello_cpu's dispatchers are Send and own the encoded paints, so the
shared cell is an Arc'd OnceLock. Without std, nothing is shared.

Measured natively with the u8 pipeline, white background plus 8px
hard-stop dots, peak heap during encode and render:

  1440x900,  20,340 tiles: 339.7 MiB, 114 ms -> 20.4 MiB, 16 ms
  1945x1894, 57,828 tiles: 951.7 MiB, 265 ms -> 45.1 MiB, 35 ms

The second row is past blitz-paint's 24,000-tile cap and only shows
the scaling. The pixels are unchanged. In kitesurf's renderer
isolate, the 1440x900 page grew the wasm memory by 431 MB before
and 58 MB after.

beep-boop-🤖

Signed-off-by: Rui Figueira <rfigueira@cloudflare.com>
